# AGENTS.md

{{synopsis}}. The owner reviews every change.
Write code that {{reader}} can read. Setup is in `README.md`.

## Checks

`scripts/check` runs every gate: the pinned tools, Ormolu and cabal-gild, HLint, a search for
switched-off checks and unsafe code, the build settings, the dependency lock, a build with
warnings as errors, and the tests. The git hook and CI run it too. It runs cabal with its own
config file, so your cabal user config and `CABAL_CONFIG` do not change the checks.

- Work in small steps and keep `scripts/check` green after each one.
- Done means `scripts/check` is green. Show the command and the end of its output.
- When a check stays red after two honest attempts, stop and report what you tried.
- Fix the cause of a red check. Gate files change only with the owner's explicit approval:
  `scripts/`, `.githooks/`, `.github/`, `.hlint.yaml`, `.claude/`, `mise.toml`, `mise.lock`, the
  warning and extension settings in `{{package}}.cabal` and `cabal.project`, and existing tests.
- `cabal.project.freeze` changes only through `scripts/freeze`, after the owner approved the
  dependency change.

Run cabal, Ormolu, HLint and ghcup only through `mise exec --` or the scripts. In a plain shell
these names can find other, unpinned copies, for example from Homebrew.

```sh
mise exec -- cabal test --test-options='--match "tidyName"'   # one test by name
scripts/format                                                # format .hs and .cabal files
scripts/freeze                                                # re-lock dependency versions
```

Sandboxed agents: cabal writes to `~/.cache/cabal` and `~/.local/state/cabal`, and mise to
`~/.local/share/mise`, `~/.local/state/mise` and `~/.cache/mise`. `cabal update`, a first
`mise install` and `gh` need the network. Without these, `scripts/check` fails for reasons outside
the code.

## Haskell

Read `CODING_STANDARDS.md` before writing or reviewing Haskell.

The checks enforce imports with an explicit list or a qualifier, extensions set only in the
`common common` stanza of `{{package}}.cabal` (no `LANGUAGE` pragmas), and the `.hlint.yaml` bans
on partial functions, `throw`, `assert` and test settings such as `withMaxSuccess`. HLint bans by
name, so names like `read`, `last` and `error` are taken: call your own `readConfig` or
`lastValue`. Only `Main` modules may import `System.Exit`. Modules of other packages with
`Unsafe` or `Internal` in their name, and their `unsafe*` functions, are not allowed.

## Layout

- Logic goes in the library under `src/`. `app/Main.hs` only calls it.
- `test/` is an hspec suite. Every `*Spec.hs` module that exports `spec` runs automatically.
  Every test suite uses this hspec-discover driver, so `scripts/check` can pass its options.
  `scripts/check` fails when a suite's `main-is` is another file or prints no hspec summary.
- List every new module in `{{package}}.cabal` (`exposed-modules` or `other-modules`). Sources
  are `.hs` files in folders git does not ignore; every new component starts with
  `import: common` and has no `buildable` field. A test helper module that is not a `*Spec`
  module must be imported by one.
- `ghc-options` may hold only the flags `scripts/check` lists: the warnings, `-Werror`,
  `-threaded`, `-rtsopts`, `-with-rtsopts=` and `-O`. Ask the owner before you need another.
- Configure mise only in `mise.toml`. `scripts/check` fails on `mise.local.toml` and other
  mise config files.
- `cabal.project` pins `index-state`. Bump it on purpose when you need newer Hackage packages,
  then run `scripts/freeze`.
- `with-compiler` in `cabal.project` names the GHC version. `scripts/check` and CI read it there.
  To change GHC, update it together with `tested-with` and the `base` bound in
  `{{package}}.cabal`, then run `scripts/freeze`.

## Writing

For chat replies, commits, PRs, code comments and docs. Many readers are not native English
speakers.

- Short sentences and plain words. Lead with the result. Then what the reader needs to act: what
  changed, what failed, what is left, what you did not verify.
- Say what a technical idea does before you name it.
- A chat reply fits one screen. Use a table for parallel facts and a numbered list for steps.
  Keep every risk and open question.
- Name files by repo-relative path.
- Docs state rules and pointers, never project status. Status goes stale.
- Commits and PRs describe the change only, with no tool or AI attribution.
- PR title: `type(scope): what changed`, at most 72 characters. CI checks it. PR body: fill
  `.github/pull_request_template.md`, one line per slot. One concern per PR, about 200 changed
  lines at most. Open it with `gh pr create --assignee @me`, so the owner is assigned. Stack
  bigger work as PRs whose base is the branch below. Squash merges make the PR title and body the
  commit on `main`.

## Review

Review each PR on its own. Before a stack merges, review it once as a whole for problems between
PRs only:

```sh
git diff $(git merge-base origin/main <top>)..<top>
```
